Workstream ID: omscs-ml-vs-ai
Title: Research OMSCS Machine Learning versus Artificial Intelligence
Status: IN_REVIEW
Milestone: Deliver a dated, source-linked course decision document
Dependencies: None
GitHub issue: https://github.com/Zanark/CareerOS/issues/16
Purpose: Compare the online CS 7641 and CS 6601 offerings using exact official
  course pages, dated syllabi and carefully qualified student evidence. Explain
  content, prerequisites, assessment, workload, practical career transfer,
  sequencing and opportunity cost without silently changing the website.
  Keep the personalized advisory document and academic context private.

Tasks:
  [x] Establish document-only scope and separate first-course versus degree choices.
  [x] Review exact official OMSCS pages and linked dated syllabi.
  [x] Reconcile curriculum revisions, semester/source conflicts and stale guidance.
  [x] Triangulate workload evidence without treating reviews as future guarantees.
  [x] Compare benefits, limitations, overlap, preparation and career transfer.
  [x] Check current online specialization allocation and taking-both tradeoffs.
  [x] Write a balanced recommendation, counter-case and practical readiness plan.
  [x] Render and inspect portable, source-linked Markdown and offline HTML.
  [x] Record evidence, limitations and same-identity tracking without private data.

Acceptance criteria:
  Use the exact online course identities, not a similarly named campus class.
  Course/semester facts have actual reviewed sources and dates. Conflicting
    or inaccessible material stays explicit; no future syllabus is invented.
  Separate verified requirements from recommendations and editorial judgment.
    Student workload reports are dated, self-selected evidence, not guarantees.
  Compare concrete learning/assessment modes, not just course titles or hype.
    Explain both the recommendation and the strongest case for the alternative.
  Consider sequencing, prerequisite preparation, degree opportunity cost and
    alternatives without treating prior completion as demonstrated mastery.
  Deliver a detailed, navigable advisory document with citations and a compact
    decision summary. HTML works offline and prints without external services.
  Keep private academic inputs, report bodies and absolute private paths out
    of issues, shared continuity and public repository documentation.
  Do not alter course selections, academic records, learning progress, website
    code or prior approved editions as a research side effect.
  Research/tracking does not authorize staging, commits, pushes or deployment.
    Preserve the local review candidate, previews and original issue identities.

